# Python Best Practices

## Project Structure
- Use src-layout: `src/manga_tagger/` for core, `src/manga_tagger/api/` for FastAPI
- Place tests in `tests/` parallel to `src/`
- Configuration is the TOML file described in `docs/00-project-overview.md`
- Dependencies live in `pyproject.toml` and install with uv

## Code Style
- Follow Black code formatting
- Use isort for import sorting
- Follow PEP 8 naming conventions:
  - snake_case for functions and variables
  - PascalCase for classes
  - UPPER_CASE for constants
- Maximum line length of 88 characters (Black default)
- Use absolute imports over relative imports

## Type Hints
- Use type hints for all function parameters and returns
- Prefer `X | None` over `Optional[X]`
- Use `TypeVar` for generic types
- Use `Protocol` for duck typing

## Database
- Use the standard-library `sqlite3` module for the library index
- Do not add SQLAlchemy or Alembic
- Keep queries in the index module, not in FastAPI routers

## Testing
- Use pytest
- Tests are hermetic: no network, no real sleep, no developer config, index, or library
- Archive tests use fixtures under `tests/`
- Call core functions directly. Do not open the pywebview window in tests

## Security
- The API binds to `127.0.0.1` only
- There are no accounts, sessions, or multi-user access
- Do not write an archive until the user saves

## Performance
- Do not add a cache layer beyond the thumbnail cache in the project overview
- Implement database query optimization
- Use proper connection pooling
- Implement proper pagination
- Use background tasks for heavy operations
- Monitor application performance

## Error Handling
- Create custom exception classes
- Use proper try-except blocks
- Implement proper logging
- Return proper error responses
- Handle edge cases properly
- Use proper error messages

## Documentation
- Use Google-style docstrings
- Document all public APIs
- Keep README.md updated
- Use proper inline comments
- Generate API documentation
- Document environment setup

## Development Workflow
- Use uv to create the environment and run commands
- Implement pre-commit hooks
- Use proper Git workflow
- Follow semantic versioning
- Use proper CI/CD practices
- Implement proper logging

## Dependencies
- Declare dependencies in `pyproject.toml`
- Install and run the project with uv
- Separate dev dependencies
- Pin versions
- Check for security vulnerabilities
